+## Learning From Acceptance Burden Transfer
+
+Treat a workflow that requires a non-coder to invent technical tests, expected
+answers or specialist QA before the development team can establish readiness as
+an orchestrator/acceptance gap. Reuse the existing product milestone owner:
+`docs/reference/CVF_PRODUCT_MILESTONE_COVERAGE_CONTRACT_2026-10-23.md`, section
+"Development-Owned Internal Readiness Before User Feedback". The team prepares
+bounded cases, justified expected evidence, required independent review and a
+real packaged consumer flow before requesting product-use feedback. Where a
+professional claim needs qualified expertise, the team prepares assessment inputs
+and asks the operator only for missing assessor/budget authority. No unauthorized
+recruitment, contact or spending; retain the affected gap until evidenced. AI
+self-certification is not acceptance.
+
+This is a reusable process rule, not a claim that a specific product has passed.
+Keep internal readiness, user feedback/business acceptance and install/publish
+authority distinct. Record the missing control and its existing owner rather
+than adding a parallel acceptance standard or transferring technical work to the
+user. Preserve mandatory criteria, safety gates and parked adoption.
+
+Apply the contract's three layers (technical verification, developer product
+acceptance, user business feedback) and five questions within existing records.
+The improvement makes product responsibility explicit; it does not assert that
+previous governance had no semantic review. Use risk- and claim-proportionate
+evidence, dated source checks, concrete value and exact delivered-workflow proof.
+Separate quality from activation/confirmation; do not introduce automatic status
+changes, parallel ledgers or per-test tranches to address the burden transfer.
+
